#X msg 130 885 @overlap;
#X text 270 885 Overlap length. When the chopped sound sequences are
mixed back together to form a continuous sound stream \, this parameter
defines over how long a period the two consecutive sequences are allowed
to overlap each other. Increasing this value increases the computational
burden and vice versa. Type: int. Units: 'ms'. Default value: 8\. Range:
[0\..100].;
#X obj 250 1003 cnv 1 515 1 empty empty empty 17 7 0 10 -216373 -1 0;
#X msg 130 1003 @pitch;
#X text 270 1003 Relative pitch change while keeping the original tempo.
Type: float. Units: 'semitone'. Default value: 0\.;
#X obj 250 1045 cnv 1 515 1 empty empty empty 17 7 0 10 -216373 -1 0;
#X msg 130 1045 @rate;
#X text 270 1045 Playback rate — changes both tempo and pitch together
\, as if a vinyl disc were played at a different RPM. Type: float.
Default value: 1\. Range: [0.1\..2].;
#X obj 250 1102 cnv 1 515 1 empty empty empty 17 7 0 10 -216373 -1 0;
#X msg 130 1102 @seekwindow;
#X text 270 1102 Seeking window default length for the algorithm that
finds the best possible overlapping location. This determines how wide
a window the algorithm may use to look for an optimal joining location
when mixing the sound sequences back together. The bigger this window
setting is \, the higher the possibility of finding a better mixing
position becomes \, but at the same time large values may cause a "drifting"
artifact because consecutive sequences will be taken at more uneven
intervals. If there is a disturbing artifact that sounds as if a constant
frequency were drifting around \, try reducing this setting. Increasing
this value increases the computational burden and vice versa. Setting
the value to *0* for the seek window length makes the parameter automatic
\, based on the tempo setting (recommended). Type: int. Units: 'ms'.
Default value: 0\. Range: [0\..100].;
#X obj 250 1355 cnv 1 515 1 empty empty empty 17 7 0 10 -216373 -1 0;
#X msg 130 1355 @sequence;
#X text 270 1355 Default length of a single processing sequence \,
in milliseconds. This determines into how long sequences the original
sound is chopped in the time-stretch algorithm. The larger this value
is \, the fewer sequences are used in processing. In principle \, a
bigger value sounds better when slowing down the tempo but worse when
increasing the tempo and vice versa. Increasing this value reduces
the computational burden and vice versa. Setting the value to *0* for
the sequence length makes the parameter automatic \, based on the tempo
setting (recommended). Type: int. Units: 'ms'. Default value: 0\. Range:
[0\..100].;
#X obj 250 1548 cnv 1 515 1 empty empty empty 17 7 0 10 -216373 -1 0;
